cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use ConfusionMapper in your research, please cite it using
  the metadata below.
type: software
title: "ConfusionMapper"
abstract: >-
  ConfusionMapper is a Python tool for classifying wrong answers in
  multiple-choice questions by the kind of thinking behind them, using the
  Confusion Fingerprint Index (CFI) taxonomy, and for checking that a human
  researcher and an AI rater agree on those labels (Cohen's kappa, weighted
  kappa, Krippendorff's alpha, Fleiss' kappa, Gwet's AC1, a 4x4 confusion
  matrix and a kappa >= 0.70 reliability gate). It implements the
  Alternative Annotator Test for deciding whether an AI can replace a human
  coder. It also writes PISA-style maths units in Indian contexts, in
  English and Hindi, with a CFI-diagnosed teacher's key, class scoring,
  item analysis and a before-and-after comparison, plus an offline web page
  for teachers.
